  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${cos}^{2}\u2061x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/768db6b8051d8f4bea528f6c5b5d386ffc0d6529517d2fe72641cb33f6e8ac10.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${cos}^{2}\u2061x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$",
